 matching fund  fundraiser uh on twitter i committed a  certain amount of money and joined with  um another woman and we basically just  went out to our our audience and um we  raised a fair amount of money and  at a certain point  um with a with a bot with the  organization that was bonding people out  but at a certain point i got a text from  a friend who lived in new york and she  said  if you have more money or you want to do  this again you should know about this  organization ift  immigrant families together and she  explained to me that this was this group  of  mostly moms in new york city who were  going the distance they were bonding  people out but they weren't leaving it  at that they were  helping to arrange transport for people  who couldn't fly because of their you  know their immigration status you know  they were doing caravans across the  country they were and my friend said you  know i'm sitting in an immigration  office right now with my daughter who  was a baby waiting for a bond  and i was like  this is incredible  um you know we all felt so powerless at  that moment and this was so tangible it  was really person to person  and then i got introduced to julie and  francisco and all these incredible  people involved and i have a friend who  is the director of this movie um linda  goldstein knowlton and i i said to ift  i'm gonna send you a donation i'm also  sending you a documentary filmmaker  because my story sense went this is this  is something people need to know about  and so  um you know we got linda to go out there  and we literally just started filming as  soon as we heard about the project and  so i think we thought it would be  you know a year or two we were literally  trying to get it out for the 2020  election  um  and um  2020 yes  it's all a blur now yeah  and that was it and that wasn't um  feasible but in a way that really  served us because the story that's gone  beyond that  period and also um now through a change  of administration it it's all the more  powerful for that all right lana now for  your involvement here what is your very  first reaction when marty comes your way  with this opportunity is it a no-brainer  right from the start given the  importance of the subject matter or does  the fact that it's your first producing  credit you know create any hesitation or  make you think i don't know if i could  do this well i feel like it's a  combination of the two you know being a  latin woman as well and being the  daughter of an immigrant of uh you know  the great granddaughter of immigrants  um  it's it's really an interesting sort of  like seeing this documentary had me  think about  how the immigration crisis has affected  my family dramatically and in turn has  affected me  being the offspring of all of them you  know so it's  it was um  i remember learning about the zero  tolerance policy uh i think it  i think it was entitled that in 2018  and we really as a country started  seeing bits of it you know on the news  in 2019 and  i was just completely horrified i am not  a supporter of the trump administration  already to begin with so it seeing that  this was happening in our very own  country was nauseating  and i  you know i i am a mother to so many i  don't have children of my own but i  love children i've raised children  and the fact that  any government can tear children away  from their families is just  ungodly  and i felt like  i had an opportunity to be of service um  jumping on board being part of this team  using my platform to raise awareness  you know  sharing this documentary with our youth  you know like i said earlier they're our  future we had a little tiny conversation  before you hopped on marty and  and um you know it is imperative that  they know what's going on in this  country and and and hopefully it will  inspire them to do something you know um  so  yes it's my first executive producer  credit and i've never you know i i've  been in scripted television and film for  so long so this is very new for me  um i am inspired and moved by all these  women including marty it's just everyone  involved  um  the courage to witness and and jenny and  and yenni  uh um and um  some people say jenny some people say  yeni but it's a it's a latin name so  it's uh my cousin's name is jesenya and  it's with a y and and jenny is the same  with the y and to see these women rosie  and jenny just  you know fighting for their lives their  families and  and the courage  you know that these women have is just  so inspiring  um so i'm i'm very honored to be a part  of this um and and i'm very excited to  see what you know where we go with it  i did have another question of the  producing of it all for you because when  i did look it up i was surprised to see  that this was your first producer credit  and it was just making me wonder have  you ever had the opportunity whether  it's once upon a time or anything you've  worked on before to to kind of jump into  that field but maybe it wasn't right but  now is the right time and then if that  was the case did this kind of you know  create that itch that you need to keep  scratching and keep producing from here  on out  well thank you for asking that i'm i've  actually been in development for the  past four years on several projects um a  couple of them haven't quite taken off  um but we have one right now that we're  keeping our fingers crossed for and i  have another one that i'm working on and  i've been an executive producer on those  and also starring in one of them so um  it you know nowadays you can't just be  an actor okay you have to be a director  you have to be a producer and stepping  out of once upon a time which was a  total like bubble up in canada  for seven years i stepped back into the  industry going uh what's going on now  you know and i and i realized like how  expansive television television has  become and you know all these women whom  i admire greatly one of them is my  dearest friend eva longoria you know  rosario dawson like seeing what these  women latin women are doing in the  industry has always been an inspiration  so yes i am i'm i'm very excited about  my new producing career  i love here and i told you before i love  seeing someone with a big platform  taking on those kinds of roles on other  projects because the truth of the matter  is some folks out there won't see these  films otherwise so it's it's a big deal  that you're committing to that and i  can't applaud it enough  um  let's talk a little bit about the  structure of the film i was i was  curious marty was this and in particular  i guess focusing on rosie and yeti's  experiences was that always going to be  the structure for the movie or did you  ever play around with other avenues to  explore  i mean in truth this was one of those um  projects where it started just super  scrappy i mean we were just like film  and see although you know wrote um  jenny's story  and rosie's story were both sort of in  progress  and what's great about you know what's  great about it is that there are um  there was footage  um before we jumped in um you know  particularly with yenni um jenny now you  got me  sorry marty  thank you  um jenny and so um  and i think that one of the issues was  like not wanting to center  um  you know ma you know the the mom so much  in ift um as  as the women who were coming over the  border and what had affected them and  their stories  you know sort of naturally came to the  forefront um but like a lot of  documentaries you sort of start with one  idea um and then it it blossoms into  something you know  just more specific or and again we  didn't think that we'd be filming for so  long  we started this in 2018 we really didn't  think that we'd be  um you know completing it now um but  it's also been so helpful to have people  like lana and um rosario and all these  incredible people who've supported us  because like a lot of documentaries  we've sort of