d um and the same was true of code  black it was much more about you know  linda worked on that as well and it was  much more about me connecting the right  people  um and you know i'm not a documentary  filmmaker um i'm a you know narrative  has you know um drama and narrative  fiction have been my bread and butter um  weirdly though my dad was a documentary  filmmaker he worked with national  geographic for  all of my life  and um and i think that i i i know that  you can apply the same sort of story  sense that i have to a situation or a  thing that feels really compelling and  and what's interesting is in both cases  it's just sort of i wasn't looking these  things were presented to me like  you know i just with code black they  were already filming but they had no  idea what to do with the footage and i  literally was at a kid's birthday party  and one of the moms that i knew told me  that her brother had was filming inside  county hospital  and could i talk to him and  then with this it was just finding out  about the the work everybody was doing  by chance  and going okay that's a story that needs  to be told especially right now so um  yeah it's not my bread and butter but i  know a lot of really amazing people who  do it and when i have the opportunity or  i feel like there's something that just  you know is irresistible i i now have  this  amazing um you know all these amazing  relationships and i can bring people  together and help them do the work that  they need to do  all right i got a big question to wrap  up for both of you here and you kind of  touched on this a little but just to  hammer it home a little harder right now  what would you say to someone out there  who says you know i've seen enough of  this on the news i get i get it i don't  need more of this this story and this  situation in my life why is this  documentary essential viewing that gives  them insight that they can't get  anywhere else in any other format well i  think that i mean  uh you know how can we not take a look  at what's happening in our very own  country you know just it's a human  crisis  like it's not just like oh it's  immigration it's really about it's  humanity how are we choosing to treat  people  you know and and that is everyone's  responsibility  so you can't just sit back idle and do  nothing about it  you have to take action on some level  because it's  where unified  be like you know  unified countries like you know it feels  so divided but like you know if if we're  going to call ourselves the united  states and the land of the free then we  actively as residents  we need to  do something to help everyone feel safe  on some level you know and um  i really truly believe when i first saw  this you know a rough rough cut of the  documentary  i immediately wanted to get in my car  and drive to the south of the border and  be like what the hell is going on and  protest and try to help and be of  service  so i i really am  hoping that the people who watch this  film  are also inspired to do something about  it and that that is the point of really  you know shining a light on these  stories and putting them out there for  the world to see  yeah and and to me  everything you said  um and also like  i think people believe they know the  story  but when you put human um  faces and experiences to that story  um  it it  you know whether you take action or not  i hope that you look at women like jenny  and rosie and you're like they should be  in this country  you know they are the kind of citizens  that we have  we have  you know built this country  with and together with and um they are  incredibly important to the to the  future of our country and the fact that  we treat asylum seekers like criminals  when  i hope i'm getting this right i didn't  know that i'm trying to cross the border  for asylum is a misdemeanor  um it's and they're treated like  you know um drug cartel members i mean  it's it's unbelievable so  if only to have